From 2fcc8b17c95200b025830f80db41886af9d71bd5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SorinS <ssuciu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 10:25:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] -g: build a program for stepping its Goose source in a
 debugger

-g (--debug-info) is --debug, plus C that LLDB, gdb and Visual Studio
debug at the Goose level:

- a #line before every line of a function's C, so breakpoints, stepping
  and the call stack are in the .goose files;
- every C local declared at the function's top, so a Goose line is one
  breakpoint location, hit once (once a loop iteration);
- no inlining unless an -O is given, so every function keeps its frame;
- a resizable's count written as it changes, so it reads right from any
  frame;
- <stem>.debug.json beside the C: the Goose names, types and scopes of
  functions, locals, globals, struct fields and enum variants, for Goose
  support in a debugger.

Without -g the C is byte-identical to before, --debug's included.
docs/debugger.md shows how to use it from the command line and editors.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
